/*  The file that makes `examples/circuits/` a PROJECT — and the first one in `examples/`.

    ★★ Its LOCATION is the load-bearing part, not its contents. A blueprint at the root of a
    tree says "everything under here is one project", and inside a project each directory is
    a NAMESPACE whose files see each other flatly. That is what lets `gates.cufe` call
    `both-of` from `signal.cufe` with nothing pulled, nothing imported and no book anywhere:
    they share a directory, so they share a scope.

    ⚠ Without this file the five programs beside it are five unrelated files, and every call
    across them is an undefined name. The rest of `examples/` works that way on purpose —
    each example there is one self-contained file a reader can open on its own.

    ★★ Running this performs NOTHING. It defines `blueprint` and calls nothing, so what comes
    out is a plan; the build happens afterwards, to that plan. Printing no output is correct
    rather than a bug — which is also what lets this file sit in the corpus and be run on
    both backends like every other program here.

    ⚠ `needs` is GENERATED, not hand-kept: `cufet pulls circuits.cufe` prints what the loader
    actually resolved. Re-run it after adding a file to this directory — a neighbour is a
    dependency now, with nothing written down to say so.

    ⚠ Paths are relative to the PROJECT ROOT — the directory holding this file — not to
    wherever `cufet build` was invoked from.
*/

Pull a book on blueprints.
    Bind series of step to blueprint:
        Define work as a series of step.

        Insert a record with (
            the name "circuits",
            the needs a series of text with ("circuits.cufe", "gates.cufe", "netlist.cufe",
                                             "parts.cufe", "signal.cufe"),
            the makes a series of text with ("circuits.exe"),
            the runs a series of text with ("cufet", "build", "circuits.cufe")) into work.

        Return work.
    Done.
Done.
